WEBVTT

1
00:00:01,686 --> 00:00:04,860
- [METAL DOOR CREAKING]
- [CLOCK TICKING]

2
00:00:04,903 --> 00:00:06,598
[CREAKING CONTINUES]

3
00:00:07,903 --> 00:00:10,511
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

4
00:00:11,947 --> 00:00:14,077
[METAL DOOR OPENING]

5
00:00:17,251 --> 00:00:18,685
[METAL DOOR SLAMS SHUT]

6
00:00:21,381 --> 00:00:24,163
- [TICKING CONTINUES]
- [FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

7
00:00:25,729 --> 00:00:27,424
[METAL DOOR OPENS]

8
00:00:27,468 --> 00:00:28,468
PRISON GUARD: <i>Open 15.</i>

9
00:00:30,511 --> 00:00:32,207
- [DOOR LOCK CLICKING]
- [DOOR BUZZING]

10
00:00:33,337 --> 00:00:35,076
[METAL DOOR RUMBLING, OPENING]

11
00:00:35,989 --> 00:00:37,902
- [FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]
- [KEYS JANGLING]

12
00:00:37,946 --> 00:00:38,946
[SHEETS RUSTLING]

13
00:00:39,641 --> 00:00:40,772
PRISON GUARD: Hey, get up!

14
00:00:41,597 --> 00:00:43,250
You got a visitor. Look alive.

15
00:00:43,294 --> 00:00:44,597
[DOOR BUZZING]

16
00:00:44,641 --> 00:00:47,120
[METAL DOOR RUMBLING, OPENING]

17
00:00:51,076 --> 00:00:52,163
Becket.

18
00:00:53,510 --> 00:00:56,076
I'm Father James Morris,
one of the chaplains here.

19
00:00:56,815 --> 00:00:58,336
How are you feeling today?

20
00:01:02,423 --> 00:01:03,467
[BECKET GRUNTS SOFTLY]

21
00:01:10,119 --> 00:01:11,293
Cheesecake?

22
00:01:12,032 --> 00:01:13,075
No, thank you.

23
00:01:13,119 --> 00:01:14,858
BECKET: You know,
I ordered vanilla

24
00:01:15,553 --> 00:01:16,771
and they brought me chocolate.

25
00:01:17,945 --> 00:01:19,032
Kill me now.

26
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:23,640
So, Father, appreciate you
coming down here,

27
00:01:23,683 --> 00:01:25,771
but I'm fine.

28
00:01:26,683 --> 00:01:28,901
Well, forgive me,
but most people

29
00:01:28,945 --> 00:01:31,248
in your position
feel a bit differently.

30
00:01:31,292 --> 00:01:33,074
Well, I'd argue
most people in my position,

31
00:01:33,118 --> 00:01:34,422
most people, in general,

32
00:01:34,466 --> 00:01:36,944
lack my sense of clarity,
presence of mind.

33
00:01:38,248 --> 00:01:39,509
FATHER MORRIS:
I don't mean to overstep,

34
00:01:39,553 --> 00:01:41,596
but, um, this may be
the last opportunity

35
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:44,379
you have to speak to anyone.

36
00:01:46,682 --> 00:01:48,335
Is there nothing
you'd like to share?

37
00:01:49,813 --> 00:01:51,857
[TICKING CONTINUES]

38
00:01:54,421 --> 00:01:56,770
BECKET: You know
why I'm here, right?

39
00:01:58,552 --> 00:02:00,857
- I know a little.
- BECKET: Well, it's not true.

40
00:02:03,378 --> 00:02:06,030
The real story,
it's much more...

41
00:02:08,073 --> 00:02:09,160
impressive.

42
00:02:09,986 --> 00:02:11,943
Well, you want
to tell me about it?

43
00:02:14,030 --> 00:02:15,030
Well...

44
00:02:16,508 --> 00:02:17,639
I should warn you...

45
00:02:19,595 --> 00:02:20,769
it's a tragedy.

46
00:02:22,421 --> 00:02:24,117
And it doesn't even
start with me.

47
00:02:24,856 --> 00:02:25,943
Funny enough,

48
00:02:26,769 --> 00:02:28,204
it starts with someone else.

49
00:02:29,681 --> 00:02:30,986
<i>And by that I mean, my mother.</i>

50
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:32,769
...has completely
forgotten how to play.

51
00:02:32,812 --> 00:02:34,769
<i>...Mary Estella Redfellow.</i>

52
00:02:36,116 --> 00:02:38,551
<i>Longtime resident
of Huntington, Long Island,</i>

53
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:42,594
<i>an heiress to a family fortune</i>

54
00:02:42,638 --> 00:02:46,029
<i>valued then at 18,
19 billion US dollars.</i>

55
00:02:46,072 --> 00:02:47,246
- Thank you.
- [INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS]

56
00:02:48,029 --> 00:02:49,246
BECKET: <i>Now,
there's a rumor out there</i>

57
00:02:49,290 --> 00:02:51,420
<i>that money
doesn't buy happiness.</i>

58
00:02:51,464 --> 00:02:53,507
Dead wrong.
Money does buy happiness.

59
00:02:53,551 --> 00:02:55,333
We're all adults here.
Let's move on.

60
00:02:55,377 --> 00:02:57,159
[SOFT INSTRUMENTAL
MUSIC PLAYING]

61
00:02:57,203 --> 00:02:59,550
<i>I'm told that the night
she met my father</i>

62
00:02:59,593 --> 00:03:01,072
<i>was a night like any other.</i>

63
00:03:04,376 --> 00:03:07,072
<i>And by that, I mean
she was young, rich.</i>

64
00:03:07,116 --> 00:03:08,680
<i>What could ever go wrong?</i>

65
00:03:10,637 --> 00:03:11,942
[KNOCKING ON DOOR]

66
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:20,941
- MARY: What time is it?
- [COW MOOING]

67
00:03:20,984 --> 00:03:22,028
MARY: Oh, my God!

68
00:03:22,071 --> 00:03:23,941
- CELLIST: What?
- Oh, my God,

69
00:03:23,984 --> 00:03:25,767
- I have to go right now.
- You gotta go now?

70
00:03:26,289 --> 00:03:27,332
MARY: Bye.

71
00:03:33,592 --> 00:03:35,331
[LAUGHING] What are you doing?

72
00:03:35,375 --> 00:03:36,941
- MARY: I have to go.
- Okay.

73
00:03:43,810 --> 00:03:45,767
MAN: <i>I applaud you
for telling us.</i>

74
00:03:46,375 --> 00:03:47,723
<i>That being said,</i>

75
00:03:48,375 --> 00:03:50,114
I think you know
what has to happen.

76
00:03:51,418 --> 00:03:52,462
I'm 18.

77
00:03:52,505 --> 00:03:53,767
Tragically.

78
00:03:54,983 --> 00:03:56,027
No.

79
00:03:56,549 --> 00:03:57,983
This should be my decision.

80
00:04:00,114 --> 00:04:01,201
This should be me.

81
00:04:04,504 --> 00:04:05,983
All right.

82
00:04:06,027 --> 00:04:08,461
You can either get rid of it
or do what you like.

83
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:11,201
But not here.

84
00:04:12,504 --> 00:04:13,635
Not under this roof.

85
00:04:14,983 --> 00:04:16,027
[SIGHS SOFTLY]

86
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:19,200
BECKET: <i>In this way, she went
from being Mary Redfellow</i>

87
00:04:19,243 --> 00:04:20,766
<i>of Huntington, New York...</i>

88
00:04:20,809 --> 00:04:22,156
[VEHICLES RUSHING]

89
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:23,678
<i>...to Mary Redfellow</i>

90
00:04:23,722 --> 00:04:25,635
<i>- of Belleville, New Jersey.</i>
- [TRUCK HORN BLOWS]

91
00:04:25,678 --> 00:04:27,243
- [SCREAMING]
- [DOCTOR MURMURING]

92
00:04:28,113 --> 00:04:29,330
DOCTOR: Breathe.

93
00:04:29,374 --> 00:04:30,852
- [SCREAMING CONTINUES]
- [OBJECTS CLATTERING]

94
00:04:30,895 --> 00:04:31,939
DOCTOR: You're doing well.
Breathe.

95
00:04:31,982 --> 00:04:33,113
MARY: I can't. No, no.

96
00:04:33,156 --> 00:04:34,548
BECKET <i>My father saw me
only once...</i>

97
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:36,547
- CELLIST: Oh, my God!
- [OBJECTS CLATTERING]

98
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:38,721
<i>...which was enough to give
him a pulmonary embolism</i>

99
00:04:38,765 --> 00:04:41,243
<i>right there at Jersey City
Memorial Hospital.</i>

100
00:04:41,287 --> 00:04:42,939
...a vehicle that we haven't
seen before...

101
00:04:42,982 --> 00:04:45,416
BECKET: <i>My mother
was left alone</i>

102
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:48,113
<i>to work at the Newark
Department of Motor Vehicles.</i>

103
00:04:48,155 --> 00:04:49,982
- [TELEPHONE RINGING]
- [INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS]

104
00:04:50,026 --> 00:04:52,373
<i>Pretty soon, she told me
something important.</i>

105
00:04:52,895 --> 00:04:54,416
MARY: In the painting...

106
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:56,025
BECKET: <i>We may have been
kicked out of the family,</i>

107
00:04:56,068 --> 00:04:58,199
<i>but we weren't kicked
out of the inheritance.</i>

108
00:05:00,242 --> 00:05:03,460
<i>See, my grandfather had
drawn up an irrevocable trust</i>

109
00:05:03,503 --> 00:05:05,025
<i>to avoid paying estate taxes.</i>

110
00:05:05,068 --> 00:05:07,025
<i>It meant that the fortune,</i>

111
00:05:07,068 --> 00:05:09,199
<i>the whole thing,
the real estate,</i>

112
00:05:09,242 --> 00:05:11,112
<i>the boats, the planes,</i>

113
00:05:11,155 --> 00:05:13,459
<i>an island or two,
would be passed down</i>

114
00:05:13,502 --> 00:05:16,025
<i>to the next oldest in line,
no matter what.</i>

115
00:05:17,112 --> 00:05:18,764
<i>And me, being the youngest,</i>

116
00:05:18,807 --> 00:05:21,112
<i>I might inherit
the entire thing someday,</i>

117
00:05:21,154 --> 00:05:22,981
<i>assuming I outlive the others.</i>

118
00:05:23,025 --> 00:05:24,981
[MARY SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY]

119
00:05:25,025 --> 00:05:26,154
BECKET: <i>I just had to wait.</i>

120
00:05:29,111 --> 00:05:30,285
For all of them to die.

121
00:05:31,589 --> 00:05:33,980
- [TICKING CONTINUES]
- [DOOR BUZZING]

122
00:05:34,024 --> 00:05:35,893
[UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING]

123
00:05:39,806 --> 00:05:41,980
BECKET: <i>Now a single mother
doing it all herself,</i>

124
00:05:42,024 --> 00:05:43,241
<i>you might assume</i>

125
00:05:43,285 --> 00:05:45,111
<i>- life was tough, but...</i>
- [DOG BARKING]

126
00:05:45,154 --> 00:05:46,806
<i>...I'll tell you now...</i>

127
00:05:46,850 --> 00:05:48,545
MARY: 'Cause they all sound
the same once you...

128
00:05:48,588 --> 00:05:50,937
<i>...despite our
financial handicap,</i>

129
00:05:51,806 --> 00:05:54,501
<i>she made sure I had all
the culture, class, and style</i>

130
00:05:54,545 --> 00:05:56,110
<i>of a highborn young man.</i>

131
00:05:56,153 --> 00:05:58,024
So when it goes,
it goes through the target.

132
00:05:59,545 --> 00:06:01,327
See how much
of a difference that makes?

133
00:06:01,371 --> 00:06:02,850
- YOUNG BECKET: Mmm-hmm.
- See?

134
00:06:02,893 --> 00:06:04,240
You just open up your shoulders.

135
00:06:04,284 --> 00:06:06,023
That is so much better.

136
00:06:07,153 --> 00:06:08,240
Okay.

137
00:06:08,284 --> 00:06:09,675
BECKET: <i>That was
the first time...</i>

138
00:06:10,762 --> 00:06:12,501
<i>the first time I saw her.</i>

139
00:06:16,284 --> 00:06:17,414
Julia Steinway.

140
00:06:20,674 --> 00:06:23,153
<i>Right off, I could see
we were the perfect match.</i>

141
00:06:24,023 --> 00:06:25,849
- [CHILDREN EXCLAIMING]
- <i>And clearly, I wasn't wrong.</i>

142
00:06:25,892 --> 00:06:27,196
<i>This was, without a doubt,</i>

143
00:06:27,239 --> 00:06:30,849
<i>a girl of great
social bearing, taste, charm.</i>

144
00:06:31,849 --> 00:06:33,283
JULIA'S MOTHER: What's next?
The big one?

145
00:06:34,239 --> 00:06:35,762
[HORSE WHINNIES]

146
00:06:35,805 --> 00:06:37,065
BECKET: <i>I was
in the right place.</i>

147
00:06:37,109 --> 00:06:38,978
[INDISTINCT CONVERSATIONS]

148
00:06:43,022 --> 00:06:44,239
What are you doing here?

149
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:47,630
Your mom invited me.

150
00:06:48,413 --> 00:06:49,543
Where do you live?

151
00:06:50,109 --> 00:06:51,196
Belleville Township.

152
00:06:52,412 --> 00:06:53,717
Do you live on the street?

153
00:06:53,761 --> 00:06:55,196
What?

154
00:06:55,239 --> 00:06:57,022
YOUNG JULIA: I heard people
there live on the street.

155
00:06:57,978 --> 00:06:59,325
I don't live on the street.

156
00:07:00,412 --> 00:07:02,065
And I mean, I won't
be there forever

157
00:07:02,109 --> 00:07:04,151
because I'm supposed to inherit

158
00:07:04,195 --> 00:07:05,717
a lot of money
or something, so...

159
00:07:05,761 --> 00:07:06,935
YOUNG JULIA:
A lot of money, huh?

160
00:07:08,238 --> 00:07:09,673
Are you trying to impress me?

161
00:07:12,238 --> 00:07:13,325
It's okay if you are.

162
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:16,543
[SOFT PIANO MUSIC PLAYING]

163
00:07:28,498 --> 00:07:29,847
[AUDIENCE APPLAUDING]

164
00:07:32,977 --> 00:07:34,760
PIANO TEACHER:
Next, we have Lyle Archdale!

165
00:07:38,847 --> 00:07:40,585
[HIGH-TEMPO
PIANO MUSIC PLAYING]

166
00:07:45,672 --> 00:07:47,063
[DISTANT THUNDER RUMBLING]

167
00:07:47,802 --> 00:07:48,846
[ELECTRICAL BUZZING]

168
00:07:49,976 --> 00:07:51,585
- [PIANO MUSIC STOPS]
- [AUDIENCE MURMURING]

169
00:07:53,107 --> 00:07:55,107
PIANO TEACHER: It's all right.
Everyone, it's okay.

170
00:07:56,671 --> 00:07:57,846
Lyle, can you see?

171
00:07:57,889 --> 00:07:58,976
Just keep playing.

172
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:00,107
[YOUNG BECKET INHALES SHARPLY]

173
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:01,150
[PIANO MUSIC RESUMES]

174
00:08:08,759 --> 00:08:09,846
[ELECTRICAL BUZZING]

175
00:08:23,323 --> 00:08:24,323
[PIANO MUSIC FADES]

176
00:08:27,714 --> 00:08:28,801
DOCTOR: Hey, Becket.

177
00:08:29,670 --> 00:08:30,758
Can you come with me?

178
00:08:34,932 --> 00:08:36,322
BECKET:
<i>Well, my mother was young...</i>

179
00:08:37,496 --> 00:08:39,106
<i>which is why
she hadn't paid attention</i>

180
00:08:39,149 --> 00:08:40,888
<i>to the symptoms at first.</i>

181
00:08:41,975 --> 00:08:44,540
- Doctors either.
- [CLOCK TICKING]

182
00:08:45,758 --> 00:08:48,670
And the family, um,
I'm sure they knew.

183
00:08:50,279 --> 00:08:52,061
I'm sure they could've helped.

184
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:54,670
But nothing.

185
00:08:57,148 --> 00:08:58,974
Not one single word.

186
00:08:59,496 --> 00:09:01,453
MARY: <i>Becket,
this is all wrong.</i>

187
00:09:05,061 --> 00:09:06,148
YOUNG BECKET:
What's all wrong?

188
00:09:08,757 --> 00:09:10,539
You should have more than this.

189
00:09:13,582 --> 00:09:15,669
Something was taken from you.

190
00:09:19,713 --> 00:09:21,930
And I want you
to promise me something.

191
00:09:24,626 --> 00:09:25,843
Will you do that?

192
00:09:27,191 --> 00:09:28,365
Okay.

193
00:09:28,756 --> 00:09:29,843
MARY: Promise me...

194
00:09:31,365 --> 00:09:32,973
that you won't quit...

195
00:09:34,668 --> 00:09:37,581
until you have
the right kind of life.

196
00:09:40,581 --> 00:09:42,060
Until you have the kind of life

197
00:09:42,104 --> 00:09:44,973
that you deserve to have.

198
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:47,799
That I raised you to have.

199
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:51,756
Can you do that for me?

200
00:09:57,451 --> 00:09:59,494
BECKET:
<i>"The right kind of life."</i>

201
00:10:01,059 --> 00:10:02,581
<i>What did she mean by that?</i>

202
00:10:04,190 --> 00:10:05,494
[CHORAL MUSIC PLAYING]

203
00:10:07,233 --> 00:10:08,277
[QUACKING]

204
00:10:10,407 --> 00:10:11,667
BECKET: <i>Her final request</i>

205
00:10:11,711 --> 00:10:14,146
<i>was to be buried
at the Redfellow Mausoleum</i>

206
00:10:14,190 --> 00:10:16,059
<i>on the family plot
in Huntington.</i>

207
00:10:17,059 --> 00:10:18,103
PRIEST: <i>We are
gathered here...</i>

208
00:10:18,146 --> 00:10:19,537
BECKET: <i>When no answer came,</i>

209
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:22,232
<i>she arrived
at her final resting place</i>

210
00:10:22,276 --> 00:10:24,929
<i>just off the parkway
in East Orange.</i>

211
00:10:26,624 --> 00:10:29,276
PRIEST: And Jesus tells us,
"I am the way,

212
00:10:29,319 --> 00:10:31,232
"the truth, and the life.

213
00:10:31,276 --> 00:10:34,450
"He who has faith..."
Or she indeed...

214
00:10:34,493 --> 00:10:36,406
BECKET: <i>In that moment,
I made up my mind.</i>

215
00:10:36,450 --> 00:10:37,624
<i>Right then and there.</i>

216
00:10:41,102 --> 00:10:42,797
<i>I would go straight
to the source</i>

217
00:10:43,797 --> 00:10:47,232
<i>and demand my rightful place
at my rightful home.</i>

218
00:10:49,276 --> 00:10:51,102
[DOORBELL BUZZES]

219
00:11:08,057 --> 00:11:10,405
- [MUSIC CONTINUES]
- [CAR DOOR CLOSES]

220
00:11:10,449 --> 00:11:11,492
[ENGINE STARTS]

221
00:11:14,275 --> 00:11:15,709
[CAR DEPARTS]

222
00:11:19,362 --> 00:11:21,404
"We hope this finds you well."

223
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:30,274
After that, I enjoyed
a brief and colorful tour

224
00:11:30,317 --> 00:11:32,317
of the New Jersey
foster care system.

225
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:37,404
<i>As for Julia,
I saw her less and less...</i>

226
00:11:39,274 --> 00:11:40,361
<i>until finally...</i>

227
00:11:42,535 --> 00:11:43,664
<i>never at all.</i>

228
00:11:45,795 --> 00:11:48,839
<i>And I had to wonder
if we crossed paths again,</i>

229
00:11:50,274 --> 00:11:51,361
<i>who would I be?</i>

230
00:11:52,274 --> 00:11:53,361
<i>A great success?</i>

231
00:11:54,447 --> 00:11:56,447
- [MUSIC STOPS]
- <i>Or no?</i>

232
00:11:57,795 --> 00:12:00,839
- [HORNS HONKING]
- [INDISTINCT CHATTER]

233
00:12:01,795 --> 00:12:04,839
BECKET: <i>Anyway, many years
later, I found myself working</i>

234
00:12:04,882 --> 00:12:07,186
<i>a perfectly respectable job,</i>

235
00:12:07,229 --> 00:12:10,403
<i>the kind where you wear a suit
to the office every day.</i>

236
00:12:10,447 --> 00:12:11,925
- Morning.
- DARRYL: You're early.

237
00:12:12,316 --> 00:12:13,403
Yes, sir.

238
00:12:14,012 --> 00:12:16,838
Uh, why don't you jump on that
order from uptown, please?

239
